The results are presented in Table~\ref{tab:loc_infill_CutPaste}. As expected, the model can easily locate the content-edited region when the bona fide subset in the training data matches the content-unedited region in the test data. In such cases, the model achieves EERs lower than 6\% on the diagonal. 
Notably, we observe a high EER close to 50\% when training on I and testing on II, indicating that if content-unedited codec-processed regions are not seen in partially edited audio during training, it becomes difficult to accurately locate the content-edited regions surrounded by content-unedited codec-processed segments. This suggests that artifacts introduced by codec processing may mislead the model if they are not recognized as bona fide during training. Crucially, when including Encodec-resynthesized data as a bona fide subset in row II, the result improves when testing on I, suggesting an approach to mitigate the misleading effects of these artifacts.


% We trained our model using various configurations, all evaluated under Setting III (with CodecFake-Encodec as bona fide and PartialEdit-Codec as partial spoof). Our results show that replacing the partially edited audio from PartialEdit with PartialEdit-Codec (row I to row II) prompts the detector to treat codec-processed segments as real from partially edited samples, resulting in improved performance (EER dropping from 47.14\%). Moreover, switching the bona fide source from VCTK to CodecFake-Encodec (row II to row III) directly informs the model that codec-processed segments are real, further reducing the EER to 5.3\%. These findings suggest that codec-processed speech can be considered real when sufficient ground truth guidance is provided.

