\section{Introduction}
Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) demand rigorous, reproducible evaluation, yet real-world benchmarking often diverges from official protocols~\cite{liang2022holistic, eval-harness}. In Korean NLP, identical models have exhibited performance gaps up to 10 percentage points across institutions~\cite{exaone, kanana}, driven by variations in prompt templates, inference settings, and evaluation criteria. We argue that true reproducibility arises not from a rigid framework but from a toolkit that flexibly supports multiple evaluation paradigms—few-shot versus log-likelihood scoring, exact-match versus generative judgment, and test-time scaling techniques—so that assessments can adapt to both model characteristics and deployment contexts.

Consider KMMLU~\cite{kmmlu}, whose official log-likelihood/direct-generation implementation in lm-eval-harness is rarely used unchanged: practitioners often augment it with n-shot in-context examples~\cite{brown2020language}, inject chain-of-thought prompts, or apply self-consistency~\cite{wang2023selfconsistencyimproveschainthought} to better capture model reasoning. 
Such customization highlights the need for an extensible evaluation framework that integrates diverse Korean benchmarks and inference backends while exposing configurable strategies for prompt design, scoring, and aggregation.

To meet this need, we present \textbf{HRET} (Haerae Evaluation Toolkit), an open-source, registry-based framework for unified Korean LLM assessment. HRET standardizes data ingestion and result reporting, supports string matching, partial matching, log-likelihood, mathematical verification, and LLM-as-a-judge methods~\cite{llmjudge}, and applies a language-penalty mechanism to discourage non-Korean outputs. Importantly, it embeds Korean-specific diagnostics—morphology-aware Type–Token Ratio for lexical diversity and systematic keyword-omission detection—to surface linguistic phenomena unique to Korean. Its modular registry design enables rapid onboarding of new datasets, methods, and backends, future-proofing evaluation as the field evolves.

We validate HRET's robustness and efficiency across diverse hardware configurations, demonstrating substantial reductions in evaluation variability and improved throughput. HRET thus offers a practical solution for reproducible, adaptable benchmarking in Korean LLM research.


