\section{Related Work and Background}

\subsection{LLM Benchmarks and Leaderboards}
The English LLM ecosystem has established robust evaluation frameworks like Evalverse \cite{evalverse} and deepeval \cite{deepeval}. The lm-evaluation-harness \cite{eval-harness} by Upstage has been particularly influential, powering their Open LLM Leaderboard which has accelerated global LLM development. Its success highlights how easy-to-use evaluation tools can democratize model development - lowering barriers for smaller research labs and startups to participate in advancing the field. Similarly, Chinese frameworks like FlagEval \cite{FlagEval} have adopted this accessibility-first approach to systematically advance their language-specific ecosystems.

\subsection{Reproducibility Challenges}

Evaluations of Korean language models often show inconsistent results across institutions, with the same models reporting performance gaps of up to 10 percentage points. For instance, Gemma2-27B and Qwen2.5-32B~\cite{qwen25} achieve scores that differ by 1–10 pp on GSM8K, KMMLU, HumanEval and MBPP when comparing the EXAONE 3.5 report~\cite{exaone} and the Kanana report~\cite{kanana} (Table~\ref{tab:gemma-discrepancy}). This variability underscores the absence of consistent evaluation practices tailored to Korean LLMs, complicating direct comparisons and hindering reproducibility.

These gaps arise from varying inference settings, prompt designs, and evaluation implementations. On top of that, Korean’s own characteristics—its rich morphology, context-dependent tokenization, and honorific system—make consistent handling difficult without standardized, Korean-focused guidelines. Together, these factors exacerbate result variability and impede reliable benchmarking across research groups.


