\subsection{Comparison with Existing Systems}
To position HRET within the landscape of LLM evaluation frameworks, Table~\ref{tab:system-comparison} compares its features with other systems. This comparison highlights HRET's unique contributions to the field, particularly its focus on Korean language evaluation and its registry-based modular architecture.

HRET differentiates itself through three key aspects: (1) \textbf{Korean-specific capabilities} including language consistency enforcement and concise morphological analysis–based output diagnostics, (2) \textbf{registry-based extensibility} that enables rapid component integration compared to traditional monolithic architectures, and (3) \textbf{unified evaluation pipeline} that unifies fragmented Korean LLM assessment practices. Unlike existing frameworks that focus on general-purpose evaluation, HRET addresses specific reproducibility challenges observed in Korean NLP research (Table~\ref{tab:gemma-discrepancy}).


\section{\hret Framework}

HRET (\textbf{Haerae Evaluation Toolkit}) addresses critical reproducibility and comparability challenges faced in evaluating Korean large language models (LLMs). By providing a standardized yet flexible evaluation environment, HRET fosters consistency, transparency, and accessibility in Korean NLP research. Figure~\ref{fig:arch} illustrates its modular and registry-based architecture, seamlessly integrating datasets, evaluation methods, inference strategies, and model backends into a coherent evaluation pipeline.

HRET is explicitly designed for: (1) researchers who require standardized evaluation methods for benchmarking Korean language models; (2) developers looking for robust tools to streamline model assessment and selection; and (3) industry practitioners aiming to reliably deploy Korean LLMs in production.


\subsection{Design Principles}

HRET introduces several innovative approaches to address the challenges of Korean LLM evaluation:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Registry-Based Modularity:} Core components—including dataset loaders, evaluation methods, scaling strategies, and inference backends—are individually modularized and managed through registries, allowing researchers to effortlessly extend the toolkit's capabilities with minimal code changes compared to traditional monolithic architectures.

    \item \textbf{Unified Standardization:} HRET systematically standardizes fragmented Korean LLM evaluation practices by providing consistent interfaces, reproducible workflows, and transparent benchmarking procedures across different research environments.

    \item \textbf{Korean Language Consistency:} Penalizes any response not in Korean by applying a score penalty, ensuring evaluation focuses strictly on Korean outputs.

    \item \textbf{Concurrency and Scalability:} Leveraging asynchronous HTTP requests (via \texttt{httpx}) and multi-threading, HRET significantly enhances throughput during large-scale evaluations, essential for computationally intensive Korean benchmarks.

    \item \textbf{Unified API:} HRET simplifies interaction through consistent Evaluator and PipelineRunner interfaces, reducing the entry barrier for both novice researchers and industry practitioners.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{System Architecture and Components}

HRET provides a unified interface for evaluating major Korean NLP benchmarks including HAE-RAE Bench, KMMLU, KUDGE, HRM8K, CLIcK, and others (see Appendix~\ref{sec:benchmark-details} for complete list), following a standardized five-stage workflow:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Loading:} The Dataset Registry unifies Korean NLP datasets with consistent formatting, supporting standard benchmarks and offering a flexible generic\_file loader for custom datasets without dedicated classes.

    \item \textbf{Model Loading:} The Backend Registry connects to various backends (HuggingFace, vLLM, OpenAI-compatible, etc), providing a unified interface that abstracts provider-specific details while supporting generation, judge, and reward functionalities.

    \item \textbf{Inference with Scaling:} Scaling Techniques enhance evaluation quality through (1) Beam Search for exploring different decoding paths, (2) Best-of-N Sampling for selecting optimal outputs, and (3) Majority Voting for enhancing stability through consensus.

    \item \textbf{Evaluation:} Generated predictions are assessed using multiple approaches: (1) String-matching, (2) Logit-based Evaluation  (3) Mathematical Verification for semantic equivalence in mathematical reasoning tasks, and (4) LLM-as-a-Judge. Additional enhancements include Language consistency enforcement for detecting non-Korean responses and Answer extraction for identifying final answers from reasoning chains.

    \item \textbf{Results Reporting:} Runtime Components orchestrate the pipeline, with the Runner combining components from all registries and the Evaluator implementing a facade pattern providing a simplified API.
\end{enumerate}

This architecture ensures consistency across evaluations while providing flexibility through pluggable components at each stage.



\subsection{Advanced Evaluation Pipeline}

Beyond the standard Evaluator API, HRET provides a flexible PipelineRunner for advanced customization of evaluation workflows. This interface enables multi-stage evaluations where outputs from one model serve as inputs to another, comprehensive model comparisons across different benchmarks and metrics, custom workflows with specialized processing steps for Korean-specific evaluations, and ensemble evaluations combining complementary models or methodologies.


\subsection{Korean-Specific Analysis Capabilities}

HRET extends beyond standard metrics by integrating a Korean-specific morphological analyzer to deliver four core analyses: (1) \textbf{Overall Performance} with detailed accuracy, sample counts, and prediction distributions; (2) \textbf{Subset Analysis} highlighting variations across task types (e.g., reading comprehension vs.\ cultural knowledge); (3) \textbf{Lexical Diversity} via Type–Token Ratio (TTR) comparisons between correct and incorrect outputs; and (4) \textbf{Error Pattern Detection} through keyword-based inference of frequent failure modes.

Together, these tools form a diagnostic framework tailored to Korean linguistic characteristics—highlighting morphological limitations and vocabulary gaps that standard accuracy alone cannot capture, and guiding targeted improvements in model training and evaluation.


\subsection{Usage Examples}

HRET provides intuitive command-line and Python API interfaces. A simple CLI example for evaluating a Korean LLM on HAE-RAE Bench:

\begin{lstlisting}
python -m llm_eval.evaluator \
  --model huggingface \
  --model_params '{"model_name_or_path": "google/gemma-3-1b-it"}' \
  --dataset haerae_bench \
  --subset csat_geo \
  --split test \
  --evaluation_method string_match \
  --output_file results.json
\end{lstlisting}

This command loads the specified dataset and model, performs string-match evaluation, and outputs results in JSON format. Additional usage scenarios and advanced functionalities are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:usage-examples}.


