\subsection{Extensibility}

Our registry-based design uses simple Python decorators to enable seamless extension. New \textbf{Models} inherit from \texttt{BaseModel} and add \verb|@register_model|; new \textbf{Datasets} inherit from \texttt{BaseDataset} and add \verb|@register_dataset|; new \textbf{Evaluators} inherit from \texttt{BaseEvaluator} and add \verb|@register_evaluator|; and new \textbf{Test-time scaling methods} inherit from \texttt{BaseScalingMethod} and add \verb|@register_scaling_method|. All decorated classes are auto-registered at runtime, allowing users to plug in new components with zero additional configuration.

To foster community-driven development, HRET actively welcomes external contributions and is committed to continuous maintenance and expansion. This collaborative approach ensures the framework evolves with the Korean LLM landscape while maintaining backward compatibility for existing users.



\section{Conclusion}

We introduce HRET, a unified evaluation framework that addresses critical reproducibility challenges in Korean LLM evaluation. Through its registry-based architecture and standardized protocols, HRET significantly improves evaluation consistency while supporting diverse assessment strategies.
HRET integrates major Korean benchmarks with multiple inference backends and evaluation methods, enabling researchers to select task-appropriate strategies while maintaining reproducibility. The framework's extensible design and community-driven development ensure it evolves with the Korean LLM landscape, providing a sustainable foundation for reproducible assessment.

%\section*{Contributions}


\section*{Limitations}

While HRET provides a standardized evaluation framework for Korean LLMs, several limitations remain. Despite integrating major Korean benchmarks, coverage in specialized domains~\cite{twice} remains limited. The language consistency enforcement may struggle with mixed-language outputs or code-switching common in technical contexts, and computational requirements for test-time scaling techniques can be substantial, potentially limiting accessibility for resource-constrained environments. Current evaluation metrics, particularly string matching, may not fully capture Korean's rich morphological variations and honorific systems, and the framework's effectiveness depends on the quality of integrated benchmarks. We are addressing these limitations through ongoing development and welcome community contributions to enhance HRET's capabilities.


