The classifier performs remarkably well, achieving an out-of-sample ROC AUC Score of 0.964 and Average Precision of 0.969. We apply this classifier to 31 million functions extracted from 80 million contributions to Python projects from the beginning of 2019 to the end of 2024 for the full population of US-based users and a quarterly sample of 2,000  users in five other countries: Germany, France, India, China, and Russia.

Figure~\ref{fig:adoption} plots the AI adoption trajectory for US developers. Adoption rates show sharp increases following major AI advancements, including the launches of Copilot, ChatGPT, and second generation LLMs. Panel~B compares the US to five other major countries in the global race toward AI adoption, randomly sampling commits from software developers in these countries. This shows that United States took an early lead, which it has managed to maintain ever since. Over 30\% of Python functions in the US were generated by AI by the end of 2024, closely followed by 23/24\% for Germany and France. India reaches comparable levels at 20\%, after having initially lagged in adoption. In contrast, Russia and China have so far remained late adopters.

Focusing on US developers, whose complete set of Python commits were analyzed, we find that AI adoption rates drop with the number of years that developers have been active on GitHub. Fig.~\ref{fig:3}b shows that whereas the most experienced developers use generative AI in 28\% of their code, programmers who have just joined the GitHub platform use these tools for 41\% of code. In contrast, using (self-reported) first-name-based  gender-projection algorithms, we find no difference between men and women (Fig.~\ref{fig:3}a).

