\begin{lemma}[{\cite[Lem.~14]{FNNS14:arxiv}}]\label{lem:ht:exists}
  Let~$\Theta>0$ and $0\leq\Gamma<1$.  Then, there exists an exp-channel~$C$
  such that every finite or infinite pulse train with pulse lengths
  $\Theta_n\leq \Theta$, $n\geq0$, and duty cycles $\Gamma_n\leq \Gamma$,
  $n\geq1$, is mapped to the zero signal by~$C$.
\end{lemma}

By choosing $\Gamma = \gamma(1+\varepsilon)<1$ for some $\varepsilon>0$
sufficiently small and~$\Theta$ so large that the feed-back loop in
Figure~\ref{fig:circuit} has already locked to constant~$1$ at time $T+\Theta$,
where $T$ is the time when some pulse $\Delta_n$, $n\geq 1$, of the feed-back
loop with duty cycle $\gamma(1+\varepsilon)$ has started, we get the following:
If SPF input pulse lengths $\Delta_0$ and adversarial choices are such that no
$\Delta_n$ reaches duty cycle $\gamma(1+\varepsilon)$, the output of the
exp-channel is constant zero; otherwise, there is a single up-transition
(occurring only after $T+\Theta$) at the output.  Therefore:

\begin{theorem}
There is a circuit that solves unbounded SPF.
\end{theorem}


