\begin{abstract}
An abstract sound is defined as a sound that does not disclose identifiable real-world sound events to a listener. Sound fusion aims to synthesize an original sound and a reference sound to generate a novel sound that exhibits auditory features beyond mere additive superposition of the sound constituents. To achieve this fusion, we employ inversion techniques that preserve essential features of the original sample while enabling controllable synthesis. We propose novel SDE and ODE inversion models based on DPMSolver++ samplers that reverse the sampling process by configuring model outputs as constants, eliminating circular dependencies incurred by noise prediction terms. Our inversion approach requires no prompt conditioning while maintaining flexible guidance during sampling.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{introduction}

Sound fusion seeks to combine an original sound with a reference sound, creating a new auditory output whose acoustic characteristics transcend simple additive mixing of the input components. In this study, sounds are classified into two primary categories: concrete sounds and abstract sounds. Concrete sounds are those that listeners can clearly associate with specific objects, scenes, events, or musical patterns that are intuitively recognizable due to their semantic content. Examples such as running water, crying children, arriving trains, metallic percussion, and burning firewood can be readily identified as concrete. Conversely, abstract sounds are those that do not reveal identifiable real-world sound events or, in musical contexts—particularly in contemporary, electronic, and experimental music clips—do not intuitively disclose recognizable patterns. In essence, the presence of describable, semantically meaningful patterns determines whether a sound is classified as concrete or abstract.

The classification of sounds as abstract or concrete is relative and contingent upon individual cognition and expertise. For instance, the calls of rare insects or distant whale songs may be unfamiliar to the general population and thus perceived as abstract; however, these same sounds are concrete and meaningful to entomologists and marine biologists in their respective fields of expertise.

This research specifically focuses on the fusion of abstract sounds. While concrete sounds offer limited potential for meaningful fusion due to their inherent semantic specificity, fused abstract sounds demonstrate musical potential that serve as material for composition.

While we use "sound" to distinguish our focus from music, this research technically operates on digital audio as the carrier medium.

The main contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose abstract sound fusion as a novel application in creative AI. 
\item We introduce a sound fusion approach that synthesizes auditory features from an original sound and a reference sound. 
\item We propose both Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE) and Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE) inversion models based on DPMSolver++ samplers that enable this sound fusion capability.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\label{review}

Inversion is a technique that captures features of real samples in referenced generation, and it is usually employed in real-sample editing, a specific subcategory of generative tasks. Unlike conventional generation processes that create content ex nihilo, editing involves modifying specific attributes and characteristics of existing samples during the sampling phase. When provided with a reference, generation maintains closer adherence to users’ intentions. 

Audio editing has evolved from earlier approaches that primarily focused on applications such as voice conversion, singing voice conversion, and timbre transfer, with research concentrated in speech and music domains. The emergence of Text-To-Audio (TTA) systems in recent years has

\twocolumn[
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1} = \sqrt{\bar\alpha_{t-1}}\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{x}_t - \sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\cdot\epsilon^{(t)}_\theta}{\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}}\right) + \sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_{t-1}-\sigma_t^2}\cdot\epsilon^{(t)}_\theta + \sigma_t\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t
\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}
]

broadened the scope of real-sample editing and enhanced precision through prompt manipulation. 

These TTA systems typically consist of integrated pipelines comprising pre-trained models, offering diverse entry points for addressing editing challenges. Models such as CLAP \cite{CLAP} and T5 \cite{T5}, trained to predict samples from text prompts, form the foundation of TTA systems and enable prompt-based editing capabilities.

Recent research in real audio editing has drawn inspiration from image editing approaches \cite{null-text, edit-friendly, pivotal_tuning}, introducing new editing tasks such as substituting sound events within audio, as well as adding, removing, replacing, and refining concrete audio content \cite{AUDIT, PPAE}. Furthermore, the research scope has expanded from music and speech to encompass general audio.

Most pre-existing inversion studies have aimed to reverse samples back to their initial latent codes \(x_T\), from which sampling begins. This technique originated during the GAN era \cite{manifold, GAN_inversion_survey, SDEdit} and facilitates various applications, including in-painting and super-resolution. From an editing perspective, inversion addresses the challenges of unexpected changes and unwanted feature preservation that occur when employing sample-to-sample approaches. Research in this area has predominantly focused on computer vision applications, with the audio field subsequently adapting these techniques, though for both domains, inversion research on diffusion models remains relatively limited.

Prior inversion approaches have worked with DDPM \cite{DDPM} and DDIM \cite{DDIM}, either approximating inversion through diffusion itself or by reversing the sampling process. \cite{edit-friendly} computed latent codes at subsequent timesteps directly from \(x_0\), and recent work \cite{zero-shot} extended this approach to the audio domain. \cite{null-text} worked with the DDIM sampling ODE and swapped timesteps for inversion approximation. Within the context of Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) \cite{CFG}, the authors optimized the negative prompt during inversion while preserving the flexibility of the positive prompt. This approach fine-tuned interpolations against diffusion latents as pivots \cite{pivotal_tuning}. However, fine-tuning embeddings for each edit incurs relatively high computational costs. PPAE \cite{PPAE} subsequently utilized this approach for audio editing.

We choose the open-source TTA system Stable Audio Open 1.0 \cite{stable_audio_open} to achieve the best possible perceptual quality. Since this system uses DPMSolver++ \cite{DPMSolver++} samplers for which no inversion methods have been proposed, we introduce DPMSolver++ ODE and SDE inversion models to fill this gap. Our approach preserves prompt flexibility, including negative prompts during sampling. Negative prompts, previously termed unconditioned prompts, were originally used as internal mechanisms to introduce variety, but now provide opposing guidance directions in many modern generation models \cite{stable_diffusion, stable_audio_2}. We evaluate our inversion latents regarding identity preservation and prompt responsiveness, and empirical evidence demonstrates that latents computed by our models achieve a balance between both objectives.

We first review the sampling process of a diffusion model in \ref{eq:1}. It yields a sample at the previous timestep \(x_{t-1}\) from \(x_t\), where \(\sigma_t = \eta\sqrt\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_t}\sqrt{1-\frac{\bar{\alpha}_t}{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}}\). Setting \(\eta = 0\) results in a deterministic DDIM scheme. Setting  \(\eta \in (0, 1]\) results in a stochastic DDIM scheme that interpolates between deterministic sampling and DDPM. When \(\eta=1\), the stochastic DDIM is equivalent to DDPM.

\subsection{DDIM Inversion}

\cite{null-text} proposed an inversion model based on deterministic DDIM. The authors omitted the stochastic term from Eq.~\ref{eq:1} and reversed the timestep direction for inversion. The latent code of the next timestep is then computed as:
\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{x}_t := \sqrt{\frac{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}}\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1} + \left(\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_t}{\bar{\alpha}_t}} - \sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{
\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}}\right)\epsilon^{(t-1)}_\theta
\label{eq:2}
\end{equation*}

They further fine-tuned the inverted latents by computing the loss against latents produced from the diffusion process, optimizing negative prompt embeddings initialized to null, \(\psi('')\).

\subsection{DDPM Inversion}
Huberman-Spiegelglas et al. \yrcite{edit-friendly} claimed that continuous noise vectors \(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t\) were tightly correlated, which led to unpredictable manipulation for image editing. They found that computing directly from \(\boldsymbol{x}_0\) during the forward process imprinted the original signal better on latents. They first computed latents at each timestep with a diffusion process, and altered the noise accumulated from timestep \(1\) to \(t\) to independent Gaussian noise:

\twocolumn[
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_t=\frac{\sigma_t}{\sigma_s}e^{-(\lambda_t-\lambda_s)}\boldsymbol{x}_s+2\alpha_t\int_{\lambda_s}^{\lambda_t}e^{-2(\lambda_t-\lambda)}\boldsymbol{x}_\theta^{(\lambda)}\text{d}\lambda+\sqrt{2}\sigma_t\int_{\lambda_s}^{\lambda_t}e^{-(\lambda_t-\lambda)}\text{d}\omega_\lambda
\label{eq:4}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_r=\frac{2r_1(\sigma_r^2+1)}{\alpha_t(1-e^{-2h})}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_t-\frac{\sigma_t}{\sigma_s}e^{-h}\boldsymbol{x}_s+\frac{\alpha_t(1-2r_1)(1-e^{-2h})}{2r_1}\boldsymbol{x}_\theta^{(s)}+\frac{\sigma_r\alpha_t(1-e^{-2h})}{2r_1\sqrt{\sigma_r^2+1}}\epsilon^{(r)}_\theta\right)
\label{eq:5}
\end{equation}
]

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_t = \sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\boldsymbol{x}_0 + \sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t, \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0, I)
\label{eq:3}
\end{equation}

Next, they rearranged Eq.~\ref{eq:3} to solve for the noise map \(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t\sim(\boldsymbol{x}_t, \epsilon^{(t)}_\theta(\boldsymbol{x}_t,\psi))\) at each timestep during sampling, given the diffused latents. To reduce accumulated error, the authors then updated \(\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1}\) through the DDPM sampling equation.

\section{Methods}

In this section, we first introduce the notation employed throughout this paper. We then demonstrate that the noise prediction term introduces circular dependency, leading to numerically unstable fine-tuning. Subsequently, we propose inversion models that reconstruct the original audio and fine-tune Stable Audio Open to reconstruct reference audio. Finally, we present the audio fusion algorithm.

\subsection{Notation and Definitions}
In DDIM and DDPM, \(\alpha\) represents the portion of the signal, and \(\bar{\alpha}\) is the cumulative product of \(\alpha\), where \(\bar{\alpha}_t=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\alpha_s\). The notation for \(\alpha\) and \(\bar{\alpha}\) varies in the original papers; however, we use them consistently throughout this paper.

In DPMSolver++, regarding the notation of timesteps \(t\) and \(s\), \(t\) falls within the range \([0, s]\) for \(s>0\). Specifically, \(t\) precedes \(s\) temporally. Lu et al. \yrcite{DPMSolver} transformed the diffusion ODE from the time domain to the half log-SNR domain, which is represented by \(\lambda\) and defined as \(\lambda := \log(\frac{\alpha_t}{\sigma_t})\).

The relation between \(\alpha\) and \(\sigma\) is given by \(\alpha^2_t + \sigma^2_t=1\). The model output is defined as follows: 
\begin{equation}
x^{(t)}_\theta=\frac{\boldsymbol{x}_t - \sigma_t\cdot\epsilon^{(t)}_\theta}{\alpha_t}
\label{eq:model_output}
\end{equation}

\(\epsilon^{(t)}_\theta\) is an abbreviation for \(\epsilon^{(t)}_\theta(\boldsymbol{x}_t,\psi)\), which denotes a network that takes the latent and prompt embeddings \(\psi\) as input conditions and outputs the predicted noise at timestep \(t\).

\(x^{(t)}_\theta\) is an abbreviation for \(x^{(t)}_\theta(\boldsymbol{x}_t, \epsilon^{(t)}_\theta(\boldsymbol{\boldsymbol{x}_t},\psi))\). It takes the latent code and predicted noise as inputs at timestep \(t\).

\subsection{Predicted Noise Introduces Circular Dependency}

Lu et al. \yrcite{DPMSolver++} proved that many high-order samplers are unstable at large guided scales, which amplified the derivatives of the noise prediction model and affect the convergence range of the ODE solver. The authors proposed replacing the noise prediction term with the model output \(x_\theta\) to achieve more stable convergence.

Taking a general SDE sampler as an example, given \(s>0\), the latent at the previous timestep \(t \in [0, s]\) is denoted as shown in Eq.~\ref{eq:4}.

Previous research performed inversion on predicted noise. However, expanding the model output with the predicted noise expression results in circular dependency. To address this circular dependency issue intuitively, we substitute the general formula in Eq.~\ref{eq:4} into the second-order SDE case and target the latent at the next timestep \(r\), as shown in Eq.~\ref{eq:5}.

From this example, \(\epsilon_\theta\) takes \(\boldsymbol{x}_r\) as its input, which is the variable we are solving for on the left side.

In this example, \(\epsilon_\theta\) takes \(\boldsymbol{x}_r\) as its input, which is the variable we are solving for on the left side. This circular dependency occurs in first- and higher-order methods for both SDE and ODE samplers. Fine-tuning on latent \(\boldsymbol{x}_r\) becomes difficult because the outer scaling factor grows extremely large during the latter stages of inversion when the maximum \(\sigma\) is set to its default value. Consequently, small error accumulation leads to numerically unstable training.

\subsection{Model Output as Constant}
\label{sec:constant}

DPMSolver++ introduced model output to replace the predicted noise term, which provides us with a sampling perspective: for every step to the previous timestep, we desire \(\epsilon_\theta\) to predict a noise map that perfectly matches the latent such that, given both conditions, the model output is exactly the same as our target sample for inversion. Hence, by assigning the encoded \(\boldsymbol{x}_0\) of the real sample to \(x_\theta\) in Eq.~\ref{eq:4} and removing the stochastic term, the latent at the next timestep \(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\) is given by:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_t=\frac{\sigma_t}{\sigma_{t-1}}e^{\lambda_{t-1}-\lambda_t}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1}-\alpha_{t-1}(1-e^{-2(\lambda_{t-1}-\lambda_t)})\boldsymbol{x}_0\right)
\label{eq:6}
\end{equation}
Higher-order integral terms are thus eliminated from the formula, where only the schedulers are considered. Apart from the stochastic term, the drift terms of the ODE variation also differ from those of the SDE. The general ODE formula is as follows:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_t=\frac{\sigma_t}{\sigma_{s}}\boldsymbol{x}_{s}+\sigma_t\int_{\lambda_{s}}^{\lambda_t}e^{\lambda}x_\theta^{(\lambda)}\text{d}\lambda
\label{eq:7}
\end{equation}

Assigning \(\boldsymbol{x}_0\) to \(x_\theta\), the integral is simplified as in the SDE formula. The latent code \(\boldsymbol{x}_t\) yields:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{x}_t=\frac{\sigma_t}{\sigma_{t-1}}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t-1} (e^{\lambda_{t-1}}-e^{\lambda_t})\boldsymbol{x}_0\right)
\label{eq:8}
\end{equation}

Here, we have inversion models for both SDE and ODE variants of DPMSolver++. We can further derive the noise map at \(t\) from Eq.~\ref{eq:model_output}:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(t)}=\frac{\boldsymbol{x}_t-\alpha_t\boldsymbol{x}_0}{\sigma_t}
\label{eq:noise_map}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Audio Fusion Algorithm}
Through inversion, we obtain latents that preserve features of the original audio at different noise levels. To fuse with another audio source—namely, the reference audio—we need to capture its features as well. Therefore, we fine-tune the model to memorize the reference audio along with its corresponding prompt. We do not require generalization from this fine-tuning process; rather, our objective is to reconstruct the reference audio faithfully from the prompt.

The fusion process is a modified sampling process. The algorithm operates in two phases: in the first half of fusion, the reference audio prompt dominates to produce interpolations with implicit features of the reference audio. In the second half, we inject the inverted latent into the sampler. 

Each term holds explicit significance at the moment of injection. As shown in Eq.~\ref{eq:4}, the first term is where the inverted interpolation is injected, while the second term holds the interpolations guided by the reference prompt. Collectively, these two terms represent an addition of two audio sources, but the third term provides flexibility for fusion. 

The algorithm uses low-order sampling at the beginning of the sampling process to warm up for higher-order sampling, and employs first-order sampling near the end of the sampling process to reduce the impact of numerical instability. Algorithm \ref{alg:fusion} demonstrate second-order sampling as an example. We ensure that the first- and second-order inversion and sampling remain consistent across timesteps. Additionally, we incorporate the CFG mechanism into the editing process. Let \(\boldsymbol{x}\) denote the latent at the current timestep and \(\boldsymbol{x}_{i-1}\) denote the latent at the previous timestep.

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
    \caption{Audio Fusion}\label{alg:4}
    \label{alg:fusion}
\begin{algorithmic}
    \STATE {\bfseries Input:} prompt of the reference audio \('C'\), \\negative prompt \('U'\),  \\inverted latent space \(\{\hat{x}\}_{\text{org}}^{i=1,...T}\), \\injection timestep \(t_j\), \\CFG guidance scale \(\omega\), \\warm-up timestep \(n_s\), \\cool-down timestep \(n_f\);
    \STATE {\bfseries Output:} fused audio \(\mathcal{B}\).
    \STATE {\bfseries Known:} noise prediction network \(\epsilon_\theta\), noise \(z\).
    \STATE Project \('C'\) to the embedding \(\psi('C')\);
    \STATE Project \('U'\) to the embedding \(\psi('U')\);
    \STATE Initialize latent \(x\).
    \FOR {\(i=T,...,1\)}
        \IF {\(i==t_j\)}
            \STATE Assign \(x\leftarrow \hat{x}_i\)
        \ELSE
            \STATE Assign \(x\leftarrow x_i\)
        \ENDIF
        \STATE Compute the noise residue \(\epsilon_\theta(x,\psi('C'))\);
        \STATE Compute the noise residue \(\epsilon_\theta(x,\psi('U'))\);
        \STATE Compute the CFG noise residue \\ \(\epsilon_\theta(x)=\omega\epsilon_\theta(x,\psi('C'))+(1-\omega)\epsilon_\theta(x,\psi('U'))\);
        \STATE Compute \(x_\theta(x, \epsilon_\theta(x))\) with Eq.~\ref{eq:model_output}.
        \IF {\(i\ge n_s\) \(\textbf{or}\) \(i<n_f\)}   
            \STATE Compute the first order \(x_{i-1}\) using Eq.~\ref{eq:4};
        \ELSE  
            \STATE Compute the second order \(x_{i-1}\) using Eq.~\ref{eq:4}.
        \ENDIF
        \STATE Assign \(x_i\leftarrow x\). 
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE Decode \(x_{i-1}\) with the VAE decoder to obtain the fused audio \(\mathcal{B}\).
    \STATE \textbf{return} \(\mathcal{B}\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Experiments and Results}

The potential for an inverted latent to reconstruct the original sample must be measured perceptually. Metrics evaluating distance or similarity between vectors, including MSE and cosine similarity, do not necessarily correlate with auditory perceptual similarity, which is our primary concern. While perceptual measurements exist for speech, these metrics are tailored to speech-specific characteristics and do not translate well to general audio. Significant differences exist between speech and general audio regarding frequency bands, harmonic content, and spectral characteristics. Most notably, speech typically occupies a narrower frequency range, whereas general audio spans the full audible spectrum and includes diverse timbral qualities, complex textures, and non-harmonic elements that speech-based perceptual metrics cannot adequately capture. To the best of our knowledge, established metrics for measuring perceptual similarity in general audio are lacking. Therefore, we employ spectrograms to provide intuitive visual analysis in lieu of numerical assessment.\footnote{All spectrograms presented in this paper are generated using
Sonic Visualiser software.} The corresponding audio is available online.\footnote{\url{https://github.com/Jng-L/SoundFusion}}

