\subsection{Original Audio Reconstruction}

To edit a real sample, text prompts serve as critical conditions to guide the sampling process, requiring a balance between identity preservation in latents and responsiveness to prompt guidance. Before fusion, we first examine the resistance of latent codes produced by our inversion models to prompt conditioning. We use null prompt embeddings in all experiments in this subsection.

\subsubsection{Necessity of Omitting Stochastic Terms}

Stochastic terms are added back during sampling because they help preserve the potential for editing. Let \(\tilde{x}_t\) be given by Eq.~\ref{eq:6}; then the comparisons are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \(x_t=\tilde{x}_t\)
\item \(x_t=x_0+\sigma_t\epsilon\)
\item \(x_t=\tilde{x}_t-\sigma_t\sqrt{e^{2(\lambda_{t-1}-\lambda_t)}-1}\epsilon_t\)
\end{enumerate}

The first observation is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:stochastic}: any form of noise added during inversion, including retaining stochastic terms or adding noise as in forward diffusion, results in a very noisy sampling phase. This observation demonstrates that omitting noise in inversion is reasonable, and that approximating inversion with diffusion produces samples of unacceptable quality with DPMSolver++. The original audio is reconstructed through VAE encoding and decoding to eliminate the influence of the VAE itself.

