We evaluate the SDE inversion model independently on the initial latent. This evaluation is based on the assumption that, apart from the initial latent representation, the model, sampling process, parameters, and other conditions remain the same. The accuracy with which the initial latent representation captures the characteristics of the original audio determines whether the interpolations in the generation process can precisely produce sound containing the original audio's features and the degree of fidelity. We compare our method with existing approaches. The comparison methods include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Sampling from scratch.
    \item Image-to-image methods from the visual domain, adapted to the Stable Audio Open workflow for audio-to-audio editing.
    \item The audio-to-audio capabilities of Stable Audio 2.0.
\end{itemize}

In the open-source audio-to-audio method, we encode the original audio through a VAE and incorporate it into randomly initialized latent representations. To ensure fair comparison, we maintain consistency across all methods using Stable Audio Open, including audio-to-audio, generating from scratch, and our inversion model. Since our objective is to examine the potential of initial latent representations, we deliberately avoid introducing additional adaptive editing schemes during the sampling process. Specifically, we do not intervene in the inverted latent representation throughout sampling but maintain methodological consistency by solely modifying the initial latent assignment. For evaluation, we employ common knowledge prompts, those with sufficient training data during pre-training, as text inputs. We apply identical text prompts and original audio (except for generating from scratch) across all comparative methods, ensuring that the same sampler and parameters are applied to maintain experimental validity.

Stable Audio 2 is a commercial version, and both its built-in audio-to-audio function and the sampling process remain black boxes to us. While it claims to have incorporated this functionality on top of the previous version \cite{stable_audio_2}, it is unclear whether it employs the same methodology as the open-source approach. Additionally, this version demonstrates improved model capabilities compared to the previous version, so we include it as an additional reference point.

There are subtle differences among the generated audio from the open-source model, and it is practically impossible to distinguish the original sound components across all five samples (see Figure \ref{fig:airplane}). As expected, audio generated by other methods should exhibit greater similarity to the original sound than samples generated from scratch; however, all outputs of Stable Audio Open sound similar regardless of the original audio content. Though the sounds generated by the open-source and commercial versions vary, both adhere to the text prompt "An airplane takes off."

As discussed in the methodology section, this phenomenon arises due to the noise control coefficient and signal strength coefficient, which make the noise guided by text prompts dominate generation during the early sampling stages. This is particularly evident in generation from scratch, where the influence of the initial latent representation is relatively weak. These findings indicate that additional controls over the sampling trajectory need to be integrated into the sampling process to achieve ideal reconstruction results.

\subsubsection{Latent Intervention Across Sampler Orders}
\label{sec:orders}

Figure \ref{fig:appx.order} demonstrates reconstructed samples when intervening with inverted latent codes at timesteps 0, 80, 120, 140, and 180, compared to the sample \(x_0\) in the last column. The three rows employ first-, second-, and third-order samplers respectively. First-order sampling is equivalent to DDIM. It is evident that features illustrated in the third-order reconstruction are relatively vague compared to the other two. Although we insert the latent at certain timesteps, higher-order methods compute with interpolations, therefore, the influence of these interpolations persists. Consequently, the intervened latents do not dominate the reconstruction to the same extent as in first- and second-order sampling. At the end of sampling, sigma, the noise scheduler, becomes very small, and the predicted noise cannot compete with the inserted latents that preserve most of the features. This indicates that initially, the prompt controls the generation process, where sigma is fairly large.

\subsection{Reference Audio Reconstruction}

The audio reconstructed through model fine-tuning restores the reference sound fairly completely, but some loss and confusion of details still exist. As shown in the spectrograms of Figure \ref{fig:ref_recon}, the reconstruction process generates a layer of blurry background noise, with the sound's stylistic features enhanced over this background noise. This phenomenon is most evident in the low-frequency portions. As we analyzed earlier, the VAE is designed to prioritize audibility and tends to smooth the regions around sampling points, leading to such artifacts in the sound. While the sound lacks clarity in details, it is acceptable overall. Therefore, we believe that the noise prediction module has acquired the capability to reconstruct text descriptions into audio with reference sound characteristics.

