\section{Introduction}
Consensus has grown among political scientists, democracy practitioners, and decision makers alike, that effective involvement of citizens in policy decisions should be regarded as a high priority for democratic institutions at all levels, from local, to national, to regional \cite{landemore_democratic_2017,matsusaka2020let}.\footnote{See also initiatives such as ``Conference on the Future of Europe" (2021-22) and ``European Democracy Shield" (2024).} Many well-established democratic innovations \cite{smith2009democratic}, such as citizens' assemblies and participatory budgeting, contribute to this civic participation goal. Yet, it is on digital democracy platforms in particular where research and development efforts are currently concentrating \cite{brill2018interactive,grossi2024enabling}, with the aim of providing citizens with effective tools that could support forms of democratic deliberation at scale \cite{pena2020innovative,mikhaylovskaya2024enhancing}. 

A wealth of this type of digital democracy tools have been developed and deployed around the world in the last decade: LiquidFeedback \cite{behrens2014principles}, Consul (recipient of the United Nations Public Service Award), YourPriorities, Decidim, Polis \cite{small2021polis}, to mention a few.\footnote{See \url{www.liquidfeedback.org}; \url{www.consulproject.org}; \url{www.citizens.is}; \url{www.decidim.org}; \url{www.pol.is}.} In different forms, and to a smaller or larger extent, all these applications provide users with the freedom to contribute statements, ideas, arguments to public deliberations on issues relevant to their communities and enable them to express their own opinions on the statements/ideas/arguments expressed by others. Supporting such processes online in large groups necessarily involves the implementation of algorithmic solutions for information-processing problems. One such problem concerns how to feed back to users information that can effectively summarize the current state of the deliberation: how does `the group' think about the issues being deliberated upon?

One approach, which is followed for instance in Polis, to aggregate the current state of the deliberation into a comprehensive summarization of it, is by selecting sets of statements proposed by users, based on how much support the statements have elicited from other users. As \cite{halpern_representation_2023} already notice, this approach can be conceptualized as an approval-based multi-winner voting problem \cite{faliszewski2017multiwinner,lackner_multi-winner_2023}, as studied in computational social choice \cite{brandt2016handbook}: users `vote' for other users' statements by approving or disapproving them. Unlike in standard multi-winner approval voting, however, information about who supports which statements is sparse in online deliberation summarization, as no user can possibly express whether they agree or disagree with every single statement contributed by their peers. For the same reason, voters can become inaccurate in representing their true beliefs, when having to answer many questions. So, such a deliberation summarization problem can be thought of as an approval-based multi-winner voting problem in which ballots are incomplete or inaccurate. From this perspective, \cite{halpern_representation_2023} focused on the problem of designing selection algorithms that could yield summarizations that meet specific forms of proportional representation proposed in the computational social choice literature \cite{aziz2017justified}, while querying users' opinions as efficiently as possible.

Our work builds on the approach put forth in \cite{halpern_representation_2023}, but leaves proportional representation aside to focus on the property of diversity. Diversity has received much less attention than proportional representation in the multi-winner voting literature \cite{lackner_multi-winner_2023}. Yet, we argue diversity is especially important for deliberation summarization. Firstly, diverse summarizations would guarantee higher inclusivity, an attribute one could desire in and of itself. Secondly, a diverse summary gives a broad, informative view of `the people's' opinion. This can be valuable especially in deliberation summarization contexts, where the number of candidates may be very large. Thirdly, cognitive diversity is argued, e.g., by \cite{landemore_democratic_2017}, to also be of epistemic advantage from a crowd-wisdom perspective. This is relevant because the selected statements typically serve as a basis for further discussions. We start from the established formalization of diversity in multi-winner voting based on the Chamberlin-Courant \cite{chamberlin_representative_1983} score and the Maximum Coverage problem \cite{nemhauser_analysis_1978,skowron_fully_2015}. Our focus thus lies on studying the computational problem of constructing summarizations that maximize the Chamberlin-Courant score, while querying users' opinions as efficiently as possible.

\section{Related work and our contribution}\label{sec:related_work}
\paragraph{Diversity in multi-winner voting and the Maximum Coverage problem}
Diversity in multi-winner voting is commonly defined following the characterization in the seminal work of Chamberlin and Courant in 1983 on representative deliberations and representative decisions \cite{chamberlin_representative_1983}. Their most general definition uses voters' ordinal preferences and, for any given committee, a voter's representative in the committee is the member who ranks highest in the voter's preference order. The committee score is obtained by summing over the individual voters' scores, attributed to them according to the position of their representative in their preference order. In the case of approval ballots, maximizing the Chamberlin-Courant score simplifies to selecting a set of candidates that guarantees for as many voters as possible at least one candidate they approve of. In 2008, \cite{procacciaComplexityAchievingProportional2008} have proven, using a reduction from Exact 3-Cover, the Chamberlin-Courant decision problem is NP complete, even for the version with approval ballots. Denoting candidates by the set of their approving voters, the approval-based problem is also equivalent to selecting a set of candidates that maximizes the coverage of the set of voters. This one-to-one correspondence between Chamberlin-Courant score-based diversity in approval-based multi-winner voting, and the Maximum Coverage problem, was first studied in \cite{skowron_fully_2015}. 

An instance of the Maximum Coverage problem, Max Cover in short, consists of a set $V$ of $n$ elements, a weight function $w:V\to \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, a family $C\subseteq\mathcal{P}(V)$ of $m$ subsets, and a number $k$. The goal then is to select $k$ sets $c_i\in C$ so that $w(\cup_{i=1}^k c_i)$ is maximized. A generalized version of Max Cover, where the aim is to maximize, by electing a set of subsets, any submodular set function\footnote{For $C$ a finite set, a set function $f:\mathcal{P}(C)\to\mathbb{R}$ is submodular if, for any $W_1,W_2\subseteq C$, we have $f(W_1)+f(W_2)\ge f(W_1\cup W_2)+f(W_1\cap W_2)$.}, was already studied in 1978, by \cite{nemhauser_analysis_1978}, who proved that a greedy algorithm is $(1-1/\e)$-approximate. However, it was not until twenty years later that the Maximum Coverage problem itself was introduced, by \cite{hochbaum_analysis_1998}, who note NP-completeness of the unit-weight decision problem formulation of Max Cover, by reduction from Set Cover, also describe the greedy algorithm, and prove the approximation ratio of $1-1/\e$. In the same year, \cite{feige_threshold_1998} gave yet another proof of the approximation ratio of the greedy algorithm, but more importantly, using a reduction from approximating Max 3SAT-5, Feige proved that for any $\epsilon>0$, Max Cover cannot be approximated in polynomial time within a ratio of $1-1/\e+\,\epsilon$, unless P=NP; the simple greedy algorithm is thus optimal.

Many years later, \cite{cohenaddad2019tightfptapproximationskmedian} showed that, under the Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\footnote{For $k\ge 3$, define $s_k:=\inf\{\delta:\exists \text{ an algorithm that solves } k\text{-SAT in } 2^{\delta n}\text { time}\}$. ETH states that for $k\ge 3,\, s_k>0$; $k$-SAT cannot be solved in subexponential time, for $k\ge 3$. ETH was first formulated by \cite{impagliazzo_complexity_2001} and would imply that $\text{P}\neq \text{NP}$.}, for any $\epsilon>0$, there exists no FPT-approximation algorithm for parameter $k$ that approximates Max Cover within factor $1-1/\e+\,\epsilon$. \cite{manurangsi_tight_2019} improved the running time lower bound from $T(k)\cdot N^{k^{\text{poly}(1/\epsilon)}}$ for any function $T$ and $\epsilon>0$, to $T(k)\cdot N^{o(k)}$, which is tight. Essentially, this shows that a brute-force approach going over all possible size-$k$ subsets is the best we can do if we insist on obtaining an approximation ratio better than $1-1/\e$. \cite{bonnet_parameterized_2016} also proved another tractability result: when we define $\Lambda:=\max\{|c|:c\in C\}$, Max Cover is FPT with respect to $\max\{k,\Lambda\}$.

In the literature on multi-winner voting, \cite{skowron_fully_2015} make boundedness assumptions on the amount of sets an element appears in. The algorithm optimizes over the $\lceil\frac{2pk}{1-\beta}+k\rceil$ largest sets, outputs a $\beta$-approximate committee in time $\text{poly}(n, m)\cdot\binom{2pk/(1-\beta)+k}{k}$, under the assumption of a maximum number $p$ of sets any element may appear in. Under the assumption that each element appears in \emph{at least} $p$ sets, the standard greedy algorithm attains an improved approximation ratio of $1-\text{e}^{-\max\left(\frac{pk}{m},1\right)}$.

Finally, a slight modification of the problem was studied by \cite{filmus_power_2013}, who present a combinatorial $(1-1/\e)$-approximation algorithm for Max Cover over a matroid constraint, using a non-oblivious local search that considers improvements of size 1 only. Non-oblivious refers to the algorithm using an auxiliary objective function to evaluate solutions. The paper also shows that, over a matroid constraint, oblivious local search, guided only by the problem's objective function, achieves an approximation ratio of only $\frac{k-1}{2k-\ell-1}$, when improvements of size $\ell$ are considered. A greedy approach strands at ratio $1/2$.

\paragraph{Incomplete or inaccurate information}
Single-winner voting with incomplete information has received some attention over the years in the computational social choice literature \cite{boutilier2016incomplete}. More recently, 
\cite{halpern_representation_2023} studied proportionality in approval-based committee elections under incomplete information. They present a version of local search PAV that queries voters and, using $\mathcal{O}(mk^6\log k\log m)$ queries, finds a solution that satisfies Extended Justified Representation \cite{aziz2017justified} and Optimal Average Satisfaction (based on the notion of average satisfaction, introduced by \cite{Sánchez-Fernández_Elkind_Lackner_Fernández_Fisteus_Basanta_Val_Skowron_2017}) with high probability. Their result extends to an $\alpha$-approximate version of both axioms, in which case the query complexity decreases by a factor $k^3$. The same study also presents a lower bound of $\Omega(m^{11})$ for the query complexity of non-adaptive algorithms, that can not adapt their querying strategy to readily obtained information. In the field of fair division of indivisible items, \cite{schafer_complexity_2024} study the number of queries required to find a round-robin allocation. When voters' responses are corrupted with a probability $p\in (0,1/2)$, the query complexity is lower bounded by $\Omega(nm\log(1/\delta)+m\log(m/\delta))$ and upper bounded by $\mathcal{O}(nm\log(m/\delta))$, in both of their two query models.

\paragraph{Our contribution}
We study diversity in approval-based committee elections when information elicited from voters is incomplete or inaccurate. We measure diversity of a solution with its unit weight Max Cover, or equivalently, approval-based Chamberlin-Courant, score. For a large part, our work combines the two lines of research outlined above, and is based in particular on \cite{filmus_power_2013}, \cite{halpern_representation_2023} and \cite{schafer_complexity_2024}. We make four main contributions. {\em First}, we adapt the local search algorithm for Max Cover over a matroid constraint studied by \cite{filmus_power_2013} to an incomplete information setting, and prove an upper bound on the number of queries required to attain a $(1-1/\e)\gamma$-approximate solution with high probability, where $\gamma$ is a parameter in $(0,1)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ls_bound}). For the original Max Cover problem (i.e. not over a matroid constraint) we study a greedy algorithm and also prove an upper bound for the number of queries required for execution (Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy_bound}).
{\em Second}, we prove, for any $\epsilon>0$ and $\gamma\in(\frac{1}{2},1)$, a lower bound for the query complexity of any non-adaptive algorithm that is $(1-1/\e)\gamma$-approximate with probability more than $\epsilon$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:incomplete_lower_bound}). Our proof follows the proof strategy of \cite[Theorem 3.1]{halpern_representation_2023}.
{\em Third}, for a setting with inaccurate information, we adapt the proof of Theorem 9 of \cite{schafer_complexity_2024}, and establish a lower bound of $\Omega(nm\log(1/\delta))$ for the query complexity of finding a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution with probability $1-\delta$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_lower_bound}). We attain an upper bound of $\mathcal{O}(nm\log(nm/\delta)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_upper_bound}).
{\em Fourth}, using real data from Polis, we provide a number of observations suggesting that our algorithms perform considerably better than what our worst-case analysis suggests. The observation appears to hold even in situations in which votes are both incomplete {\em and} inaccurate. For an overview of the theoretical results, see Figure~\ref{fig:tree}.

