\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}
An instance of an approval-based committee election problem consists of a set $V$ of $n$ voters, a set $C$ of $m$ candidates, and a natural number $k$. The goal is to elect a committee $W\subseteq C$ of size $k\le m$, based on the opinions of the voters in $V$. In the context of deliberation summarization, we often even have $k\ll m$. An instance also contains approval information: every voter $i\in V$ approves of a subset of the candidates. Usually, this approval information is known upfront and expressed in the form of an \emph{approval set} $A_i$ for each voter $i\in V$. The sequence of sets $A=(A_1,\ldots,A_n)$ is called the \emph{approval profile}, and we call this setting the \emph{perfect information setting}. This paper studies incomplete and inaccurate information settings; the respective models are detailed in the corresponding section.
