To measure diversity of an elected committee, we use the approval-based Chamberlin-Courant score, which is equivalent to the unit weight Max Cover score: % We can define any candidate $c\in C$ by the set of its approvers so then $C\subseteq \mathcal{P}(V)$ and the instance is fully defined by $(V,C,k)$.
\begin{definition}[\cite{chamberlin_representative_1983}]
On any approval-based committee election instance $(V,C,A,k)$, the \emph{approval-based Chamberlin-Courant} (CC) score of a committee $W\subseteq C,\,|W|=k$ is
\begin{align}
\text{CC}(W)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathds{1}_{\{A_i\cap W\neq\emptyset\}}(i).
\end{align}
In the approval-based Chamberlin-Courant decision problem, the input is an instance $(V,C,A,k)$ and an integer $x$, and the question is whether it is possible to achieve score $\frac{x}{n}$ using $k$ candidates. In the approval-based Chamberlin-Courant optimization problem, the input is an instance $(V,C,A,k)$ and the task is to maximize the score using $k$ candidates.
\end{definition}

As mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:related_work}, the approval-based Chamberlin-Courant decision problem is NP-complete, so it is not possible to, on just any instance, find an optimal solution to the optimization problem in polynomial time either (unless P=NP). To find a solution in polynomial time, we thus have to work with approximation algorithms. The performance of approximation algorithms is defined in terms of the \emph{approximation ratio}. An algorithm $\alg$ for a maximization problem $\Pi$ with objective function $f$ has approximation ratio $\alpha$ if for any instance $I$, $f(\alg(I))\ge f(\opt(I))\alpha$, where $\opt(I)$ denotes an optimal solution for $I$. The best attainable polynomial-time approximation ratio for Max Cover is $1-1/\e$ \cite{feige_threshold_1998}.

Since we study only approval-based committee elections, and the approval-based Chamberlin-Courant problem is equivalent to the unit-weight Maximum Coverage problem, we use both terms interchangeably. Similarly, we refer interchangeably to voters/elements, to candidates/sets, and committees/covers. Whenever we speak about the Chamberlin-Courant problem, the approval-based version is meant, and whenever the Maximum Coverage problem is mentioned, the unit-weight version is meant.

\section{Incomplete information}
In the incomplete information setting, voters are only able to express their votes (approve/disapprove) on a small portion of the candidates, so we don't have access to all approval information beforehand. To model this, we assume there exists an underlying true distribution $d$ of approval preferences, and we now write $A:V\to \mathcal{P}(C)$ for a random variable, distributed according to $d$, that maps a voter $i$ to the set $A(i)$ of candidates that voter $i$ approves of. To obtain approval information, an algorithm must draw samples from $d$. We assume that voters are able to respond to at most $t$ approve/disapprove questions at one go, so drawing voter $i$, we obtain, for some set $Q\subseteq C$ for which $|Q|=t$, the set $A(i)\cap Q$. We call the voter-candidate pair $(i,j)$ a \emph{query} and $Q$ a \emph{query set}. This means query set $Q$ allows us to perform $t$ queries per queried voter. % Any voter's preferences are only revealed upon querying them, at which point we draw their preferences (restricted to the queried set) from $d$.
We are interested in finding a solution to Max Cover in this incomplete information setting. When voters \emph{are} able to express their vote on all candidates, $nm$ query responses suffice to gather all information, and we will surely find a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution, e.g. with a greedy approach. When voters are \emph{not} able to express their vote on all candidates, the algorithm's performance might drop. In this section, we present theoretical results on the relation between the approximation ratio and the number of queries required to obtain that ratio. We study two settings. Firstly, the original problem, where we may elect any $k$-sized committee we want, and, secondly, the problem over a matroid constraint, where the elected committee has to be part of a matroid that is defined with the problem instance. For the proof of a lower bound for the query complexity of non-adaptive algorithms in the original problem, we refer to the appendix.

\subsection{Upper bound for the unconstrained setting}\label{sec:gr}
\paragraph{The standard greedy algorithm}
We prepare the ground by considering the perfect information setting. Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy} is the greedy algorithm for this setting. % We write $c\in C$ as a set of voters, namely those that approve of $c$, so $c\in\mathcal{P}(V)$. We thus merge candidates $c$ and $c'$ that have the same set of approving voters: once $c\in W$, the marginal increase in score of adding $c'$ to $W$ is zero, so the greedy algorithm would not elect $c'$ over any other yet unelected candidate. 
We write $\Delta(W,c')$ for the increase in Chamberlin-Courant score obtained by adding $c'$ to committee $W$. Here, but also in future algorithms, we abuse notation by using $\in$ for lists.

