For the incomplete information setting, that we discuss in detail in the next section, we estimate approval information of the total population, based on the information of only few voters. This can lead to errors. Write $\gr\hspace{-0.5mm}\text{-}\epsilon$ for an adaptation of Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries} that, in each iteration, might choose any candidate that yields the largest increase in score \emph{up to $-\epsilon$}. %\todo{alg in appendix} 
We can compute the approximation ratio in a similar fashion as before in Lemma~\ref{lem:greedy_cc_ratio}. 
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:greedy_eps}
For any non-trivial instance, \textsc{greedy}-$\epsilon$ is $\left(\left(1-\frac{1}{\e}\right)\cdot\left(\frac{1}{1+\epsilon(1-\frac{1}{\e})}\right)\right)$-approximate.
\end{lemma}

\paragraph{The greedy query algorithm}
We now turn to the incomplete information setting. Recall that here we assume there is an underlying unknown distribution $d$ of voter preferences and we write $A:V\to \mathcal{P}(C)$ for the random variable that maps a voter $i$ to its approval set $A(i)$, and is distributed according to $d$. In the query model, we repeatedly sample sets (of size $\ell\le n$) of voters independently from $d$ and ask these voters for their votes on some query set $Q\subseteq C$ (of size $t$). Querying any set $Q\subseteq C$, on \emph{all} voters, the responses allow us to compute, for any $S\subseteq Q$,  
\begin{align}
p_S := \P(A\cap S\neq\emptyset)= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathds{1}(A(i)\cap S\neq \emptyset);
\end{align}
the Chamberlin-Courant score of $S$. We can now write $\Delta(W,c') := p_{W\cup \{c'\}}-p_{W}$, and we can compute this with complete information on a query containing $W\cup\{c'\}$. Sampling $\ell$ voters from $d$ independently and presenting them with query set $Q\subseteq C$, we can compute, for every subset $S\subseteq Q$,
\begin{align}
\hat{p}_S := \frac{1}{\ell}\sum_{i=1}^\ell\mathds{1}(\{A(i)\cap S\neq\emptyset\}),
\end{align}
that estimates $p_S$. We write $\hat{\Delta}(W,c')=\hat{p}_{W\cup \{c'\}}-\hat{p}_{W}$ for an estimate of $\Delta(W,c')$ based on values $\hat{p}$. Using this querying approach, we obtain Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries}. Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy_bound} gives an upper bound on the amount of query sets required to run Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries}.


\begin{algorithm}[h]
	\SetAlgoNoLine
	\KwIn{Numbers $n,m,k\in\N$ with $k\le m$, set $V$ of $n$ voters, set $C\subseteq\mathcal{P}(V)$ of $m$ candidates, query size $t$, with $m\ge t>k$, and $\gamma\in(0,1),\ \delta\in(0,1]$.}
	\KwOut{Committee $W\subseteq C$ of size $k$.}
    Set $\epsilon=\frac{(1-\gamma)e}{\gamma(e-1)}$ and $\ell=\lceil \frac{2}{\epsilon^2}(\log(\frac{2mk}{\delta}))\rceil$\;
    Let $W=[\ ]$ be an empty list\;
    \For{$i=1,\ldots,k$,}{
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Construct a set of query sets $\mathcal{Q}=\{Q_i\}_i$ with $Q_i\subseteq C$ and $|Q_i|=t$ for all $i$, and such that $W\subseteq\bigcap\mathcal{Q}$ and $\bigcup\mathcal{Q}=C$, and present each query set to $\ell$ voters.
			\item  For all $c'\notin W$, determine $\hat{\Delta}(W,c')$ as an estimate of $\Delta(W,c')$ using $\ell$ voters and query set $Q$ containing $W\cup c'$.
			\item Append $W$ with  $\argmax_{c'\notin W}\,\hat{\Delta}(W,c')$.
		\end{enumerate}
	}
    \caption{\textsc{Greedy-queries}}
    \label{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:greedy_bound}
For any non-trivial instance, any $\delta>0,\, \gamma\in(0,1)$, and $k<t\le m$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries} is $(1-1/\e)\gamma$-approximate for Max Cover using 
\[
k\cdot t \left\lceil\frac{m-k}{t-k}\right\rceil\cdot\left \lceil 0.8\left(\frac{\gamma}{1-\gamma}\right)^2\log\left(\frac{2 m k}{\delta}\right)\right \rceil\in 
\mathcal{O}\left(\left(\frac{\gamma}{1-\gamma}\right)^2km\log\left(\frac{km}{\delta}\right)\right)
\]
queries.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
For $m=1000,\, \gamma=0.85,\,\delta=0.05,\,k=8$ and $t=20$, the upper bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy_bound} gives 432 920 queries.
\end{example}

\subsection{Upper bound for a setting with matroid constraints}
\paragraph{Matroids in multi-winner voting}
We can imagine situations where we want to place restrictions on the set of possible committees to be elected. For instance, perhaps we want to include `external' diversity requirements, such as `the committee must contain at least $x$ candidates of category $Y$'. In practice, $x$ would be a natural number and $Y$ would be a category like gender, race, etc. In online deliberation settings, these attributes would extend to users. Or perhaps we want to place a restriction on how many comments can be selected that are submitted by one and the same person. For such purposes, we make a selection of which committees are “allowed” to be elected, and collect them in a set  $\mathcal{M}$. This is fundamentally different from what we have so far meant with diversity, which was just defined in terms of the approval profile. It is not obvious that any algorithm adapts well to this restriction. Fortunately, \cite{filmus_power_2013} prove that, when $\mathcal{M}$ is a matroid, we can mend this problem.

\begin{definition}[matroid]\label{matroid}
A \textit{matroid} $\mathcal{M}$ over a set $C$ is a selection of subsets (called independent sets) that satisfies
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $\emptyset\in\mathcal{M}$,
	\item if $A\in\mathcal{M}$ and $B\subset A$ then $B\in\mathcal{M}$, and
	\item for all $A,B\in\mathcal{M}$ with $|A|>|B|$, there exists $x\in A\setminus B$ such that $B\cup\{x\}\in\mathcal{M}$.	
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
This last property guarantees that all maximal independent sets have the same cardinality. We call such sets \emph{bases}, and their common cardinality is called the \emph{rank} of the matroid.

\begin{example}[uniform matroid]
Consider $\mathcal{M}=\{W\subset C:|W|\le k\}$. This is the uniform matroid of rank $k$: 1) $|\emptyset|=0\le k$, 2) for any set $W\in\mathcal{M}$ with $|W|=j\le k$, for any subset $W'\subseteq W$, $|W'|\le|W|\le k$, and 3) for any two feasible sets $W,\ W'\in\mathcal{M}$ with $|W'|<|W|$, we will have, for any $c\in W\setminus W'$, that $|W'\cup \{c\}|\le k$.
\end{example}
Thus, taking for $\mathcal{M}$ the uniform matroid of rank $k$, we retrieve the original problem. This shows the problem with matroid constraints is a generalization of the original problem, hence more difficult.

\begin{example}[a matroid implementing quota]\label{ex:quota}
Define a set of bases $M=\{W\subseteq C:|W|= k,\ \ |W\cap Y|=\frac{1}{2}k,\ |W\cap X|=\frac{1}{2}k\}$ and consider $\mathcal{M}=M \bigcup \ \{V:V\subseteq W,\ W\in M\}$. This is a matroid of rank $k$.
\end{example}
In Example~\ref{ex:quota}, only the bases elements comply with the quota. This is not a problem, we show next.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:matroid}
For any instance $(V,C,A,k)$ and any matroid $\mathcal{M}$ of rank $k$, no cover of size $\ell<k$ will attain a unique maximal Max Cover score.
\end{proposition}

Thus, there is always basis among those covers with the highest score. When an algorithm does output a cover $L$ of size $\ell<k$, we can take any basis $K$ and add elements from $K$ to $L$ until it has rank $k$.

\paragraph{The standard non-oblivious local search algorithm}
As done above, we start with the complete information setting. Here, a classical (or, oblivious) local search algorithm (for Max Cover) typically starts with an arbitrary (tentative) solution and, in each iteration, swaps one or multiple of the tentatively elected sets for tentatively unelected sets in order to improve the objective function value. It thus \textit{searches} in the \textit{local} neighborhood of the solution. It stops when no local improvement is possible anymore, which could in principle take exponentially many steps. As described in \cite{filmus_power_2013}, this running time problem can be circumvented. We can use approximate local search, which allows us to bound the running time at a small cost in the approximation ratio, see also \cite{orlin_approximate_2004}. In practice, this means we stop the algorithm sooner, namely when we encounter, in some iteration, an improvement smaller than some parameter $\beta>0$. Then, using a partial enumeration technique, we can eliminate this small cost again, see \cite{calinescu_maximizing_2011}. This is also the approach that \cite{filmus_power_2013} take. %\todo{ I don't understand their run time analysis of the partial enumeration version (why is s not n?) i would say multiply by the amount of candidates. argue that this is not worth it for our queries?}

\cite{filmus_power_2013} prove that, over a matroid constraint, the greedy algorithm would attain only approximation ratio $\frac{1}{2}$. Also, the approximation ratio of oblivious local search (Algorithm 1 in their paper)%\todo{appendix}
is $\frac{k-1}{2k-\ell-1}$ when $\ell$ sets are exchanged in each iteration. For $k=2$ and $\ell=1$, this also equals $\frac{1}{2}$, which is quite a lot smaller than $1-1/\e$. Thus, they switch to a non-oblivious local search algorithm that uses an auxiliary objective function $f$ for the iterative procedure. This auxiliary objective function adds temporary weight to elements covered multiple times. This makes a path of improvements more “robust”, and thereby prevents getting stuck in bad local optima. We denote by $\alpha_j$ the temporary weight associated to an element covered $j$ times, and write $h_i(W)$ for the amount of times that $i$ is covered by $W$. Then, the auxiliary objective function value of committee $W$ is
\begin{align}
f(W)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_{h_i(W)}.
\end{align}
Note that, with $\alpha_0=0$ and $\alpha_j=1$ for all $j>0$, we retain the original oblivious objective function, but if we set $\alpha_j>\alpha_1$ for $j>1$, we add additional weight to elements covered multiple times. As in \cite{filmus_power_2013}, we define
\begin{align}
\alpha_0=0,\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \alpha_1=1-\frac{1}{\e},\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \alpha_{j+1}=(j+1)\alpha_j-j\alpha_{j-1}-\frac{1}{\e}.
\end{align}
The sequence $(\alpha_n)_{n\in\N_{0}}$ thus gives zero points to a voter that is not covered by the committee at all, $1-\frac{1}{\e}\approx 0.632$ points to a voter that is covered by a single committee member, $2-\frac{3}{\e}\approx 0.896$ points to a voter that is covered twice, and so on. \cite{filmus_power_2013} prove this choice for the sequence $(\alpha_n)_{n\in\N_{0}}$ is optimal and that, for any $\gamma>0$, using this auxiliary objective function, the non-oblivious local search algorithm, with parameter $\beta$ (decreasing in $\gamma$), is $(1-1/\e-\gamma)$-approximate.

We have adjusted the non-oblivious local search algorithm of \cite{filmus_power_2013} to suit our setting better. We explain the changes below (in Section~\ref{sec:ls-query-alg}), as they are induced by the querying strategy. For now it is only important to know that the changes do not affect the approximation guarantees of the algorithm. See our adapted version, Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS}, below, where, for $c\in W$ and $c'\notin W$, we now write $\Delta(W,c'):=f(W\cup \{c'\})-f(W)$ and $\Delta(W,c',c):=f((W\cup \{c'\})\setminus \{c\})-f(W)$.

\begin{algorithm}[h]
	\SetAlgoNoLine
	\KwIn{Numbers $n,m,k\in\N$ with $k\le m$, set $V$ of $n$ voters, set $C$ of $m$ candidates, $\beta>0$.}
	\KwOut{Committee $W$, consisting of $k$ candidates.}
    Choose $W\subset C$ such that $|W|=k$, and $c\in W$ and $c'\notin W$ so that $(W\cup \{c'\}) \setminus \{c\}\in\mathcal{M}$\;
    \Repeat{$\Delta(W,c',c)\le\beta$ and $f(W)>1/n$}{
    \begin{enumerate}
          \item $W=(W\cup \{c'\}) \setminus \{c\}$.
	       \item Let $\mathcal{E}$ be the set of all valid exchanges for $W$ according to $\mathcal{M}$.
	       \item Pick $(c',c)\in\argmax_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{E}}\Delta(W,x,y)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    }
    \caption{\textsc{Local Search}-$\beta$}
    \label{alg:LS}
\end{algorithm}

\cite[Corollary 6]{filmus_power_2013} prove that the local search algorithm with parameter $\beta=\frac{\gamma}{C_0 k\log k(1-1/\e-\gamma)}$, is $(1-1/\e-\gamma)$-approximate for any $\gamma>0$ and some universal constant $C_0$ for which (by their Lemma 4) we know that $\alpha_i \le C_0\log i$ for all $i\le k$. Simplifying notation, we write $\beta=C_1\frac{\gamma}{k\log k}$ for any $\gamma>0$ and some universal constant $C_1 = \frac{1}{C_0(1-1/\e-\gamma)}\le \frac{\log i}{\alpha_i(1-1/\e-\gamma)}$ for any $i\le k$.

\paragraph{The local search query algorithm}\label{sec:ls-query-alg}
We turn to the incomplete information setting. Like before, we write $A:V\to \mathcal{P}(C)$ for the random variable, with unknown distribution $d$, that maps a voter $i$ to approval set $A(i)$. After querying any set $Q\subseteq C$ on \emph{all} voters, we can compute, for any $S\subseteq Q$,
\begin{align}
p_S :=\P(A\cap Q=S) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathds{1}(A(i)\cap Q=S),
\end{align}
for the probability that a voter approves, within the set $Q$, of exactly the set $S$. Note that this is different than how $p_S$ was defined in Section~\ref{sec:gr}, but both are meant to denote the relevant information that we attain from a query set $Q$. With this information, we can compute $f(Q)$. Since this time, we have, for $c'\notin W$ and $c\in W$, $\Delta(W,c'):= f(W\cup\{c'\})-f(W)$ and $\Delta(W,c',c):=f((W\cup\{c'\})\setminus\{c\})-f(W)$, we can compute these values with complete information on a query set containing $W\cup\{c'\}$. Indeed,
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
f(W)&= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_{h_i(S)}\\
&= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{S\subseteq W}\mathds{1}(A(i)\cap W=S)\cdot\alpha_{|S|}\\
&= \sum_{S\subseteq W}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathds{1}(A(i)\cap W=S)\cdot\alpha_{|S|}\\
&= \sum_{S\subseteq W}p_S\cdot \alpha_{|S|}.
\end{split}
\end{align}
Sampling $\ell\le n$ voters from $d$ independently and presenting them with query set $Q\subseteq C$, we can compute, for every subset $S\subseteq Q$,
\begin{align}
\hat{p}_S := \frac{1}{\ell}\sum_{i=1}^\ell\mathds{1}(A(i)\cap Q=S),
\end{align}
that estimates $p_S$. Consequently, we write $\hat{\Delta}(W,c') = \hat{f}(W\cup\{c'\})-\hat{f}(W) =\sum_{S\subseteq W\cup\{c'
\}}\hat{p}_S\cdot \alpha_{|S|}-\sum_{S\subseteq W}\hat{p}_S\cdot \alpha_{|S|}$ for an estimate of $\Delta(W,c')$ based on the values $\hat{p}$) and $\hat{\Delta}(W,c',c)$ as an estimate of $\Delta(W,c',c)$ based on values $\hat{p}$ analogously. We present our querying algorithm in Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}.

\begin{algorithm}[h]
	\SetAlgoNoLine
	\KwIn{Numbers $n,m,k\in\N$ with $k\le m$, set $V$ of $n$ voters, set $C$ of $m$ candidates, $\beta>0$, query size $t$, with $m\ge t>k$, constants $\delta\in(0,1]$ and $\xi\ge 1$.}
	\KwOut{Committee $W$, consisting of $k$ candidates.}
    $\epsilon = \frac{\xi - 1}{2\xi}\cdot \beta$, $\ell=\left\lceil\frac{(2-2/\e)^2}{2\epsilon^2}\log\left(\frac{2\cdot(m-k)k\cdot\xi\alpha_k}{\delta\cdot\beta}\right)\right\rceil$\;
    Choose $W\subset C$ such that $|W|=k$, and $c\in W$ and $c'\notin W$ so that $(W\cup \{c'\}) \setminus \{c\}\in\mathcal{M}$\;
    \Repeat{$\hat{\Delta}(W,c',c) < \beta -\epsilon$ and $\hat{f}(W)>\alpha_1/n$}{
    \begin{enumerate}
           \item $W=(W\cup \{c'\}) \setminus \{c\}$;
	       \item Let $\mathcal{E}$ be the set of all valid exchanges for $W$ according to $\mathcal{M}$.
          \item Construct a set of query sets $\mathcal{Q}=\{Q_i\}_i$ with $Q_i\subseteq C$ and $|Q_i|=t$ for all $i$, and such that $W\subseteq\bigcap\mathcal{Q}$ and $\bigcup\mathcal{Q}=C$, and present each query set to $\ell$ voters.
	       \item For all $c\in W,\, c'\notin W$, determine $\hat{\Delta}(W,c',c)$ as an estimate of $\Delta(W,c',c)$ using $\ell$ voters and query set $Q$ containing $W\cup \{c'\}$.
	       \item Pick $(c',c)\in\argmax_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{E}}\hat{\Delta}(W,x,y)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    }
    \caption{\textsc{Local Search}-$\beta$-\textsc{queries}}
    \label{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}
\end{algorithm}

As previously mentioned, in Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS} (hence also Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}) we implemented several changes with respect to Algorithm 2 of \cite{filmus_power_2013}. We explain them here. There are three changes. Firstly, we have left out the greedy initialization. Although it speeds up the convergence time-wise, it would cost many extra querying rounds. It is not possible to query only for the initialization and use the gathered information for the local search phase, since candidates not elected in the initialization, or only elected in its last round, will not have query responses from all voters whereas that is necessary if the candidate would become part of the tentative solution at some point during the local search. However, it might be possible to save the information gathered in the initialization in a smart way. For now, we leave out this phase. For similar reasons, we omit the partial enumeration step that would yield the clean $1-1/\e$ ratio. Secondly, we have adapted the stopping condition from $\frac{f((W\cup c')\setminus c)}{f(W)}\le 1+\beta n$ (the factor $n$ is added because we use relative scores) to $f((W\cup c')\setminus c)-f(W)\le\beta$. Before we can make any kind of statement about the performance of our algorithm, we need guarantees on the accuracy of our estimations. In order to use Hoeffding's inequality to bound the probability that our estimates are far from the estimated value, we need our samples to be bounded as well. However, since $\alpha_0=0$, the expression $\frac{f((W\cup c')\setminus c)}{f(W)}$ is unbounded. Suppose now, that $f((W\cup c')\setminus c)-f(W)\le\beta$, then 
\begin{align}
f((W\cup c')\setminus c)-f(W)\le \beta\implies \frac{f((W\cup c')\setminus c)}{f(W)}\le 1+\frac{\beta}{f(W)},
\end{align}
which is at most $1+\beta n$ iff $f(W)\ge 1/n$. This might not be true immediately, but will be true at some point as long as $\sum_{i\in V}\mathds{1}(|A(i)|\ge 1)\ge 1$, so also when the instance is non-trivial. Thus, we also add the requirement that $\hat{f}(W)\ge \alpha_1/n$, and then our adapted stopping  condition is a stronger requirement for any non-trivial instance. This makes our third adaptation. Theorem~\ref{thm:ls_bound} gives the amount of queries required for Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}. As expected, this amount is higher than stated in Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy_bound} for the original problem.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ls_bound}
For any $\delta>0$, $\gamma\in(0,1)$, $m\ge t > k$, and some universal constant $C_2$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}, with $\beta = C_2\frac{1-\gamma}{\gamma k\log k}$, is $(1-1/\e)\gamma$-approximate for Max Cover when using 
\[
\xi\alpha_k\cdot\left(\frac{\gamma k\log k}{C_2(1-\gamma)}\right)^3\cdot t\left\lceil\frac{m-k}{t-k}\right\rceil\cdot\left\lceil\frac{(2-2/\e)^2}{2}\left(\frac{2\xi}{\xi-1}\right)^2\log\left(\frac{2\cdot(m-k)k\cdot\xi\alpha_k\cdot\gamma  k\log k}{C_2(1-\gamma)\delta}\right)\right\rceil
\]
queries. Asymptotically in $k,m,\gamma$ and $\delta$, this is in
\[
\mathcal{O}\left(\left(\frac{\gamma k\log k}{1-\gamma}\right)^3m\log\left(\frac{m\gamma k^2\log k}{\delta(1-\gamma)}\right)\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
The number of queries increases in $\xi$ when $\xi\ge3$, and has a negative asymptote at $\xi=1$, so regardless of the values of the other parameters, we attain a minimum for some $\xi\in[1,3]$. Fixing $\xi=3$, for $m=1000,\,\delta=0.05,\,k=8$ and $t=20$ the upper bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:ls_bound} gives $9.52 180\cdot 10^{11}$ queries; quite a lot.
\end{example}

\section{Inaccurate information}
In the inaccurate information setting, voters do hand in complete ballots, but the voters are inaccurate in reporting their true approvals: each time we query a voter $i$ about a candidate $j$ (so, posing query $(i,j)$), the response is corrupted with probability $p$. We model this by adding, to the \emph{original} complete information approval profile $A$, a random variable $X\sim\text{Bernoulli}(p)$, with $p\in(0,\frac{1}{2})$. Posing query $(i,j)$, we also draw from $\text{Bernoulli}(p)$, and the result determines whether we see a truthful response (draw 0) or not (draw 1). Draws from $X$ are independent between voters, between candidates, and between consecutive draws for the same query. If $p=0$, we would have accurate information, and with $nm$ queries we would have all, accurate, information. We would then be sure to find a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution (e.g. using the greedy algorithm). Now $p>0$, so without adapting the algorithms, their performance will drop. We here present theoretical results on the amount of queries required to still obtain a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution with high probability.

When answers may be corrupted, we can simply pose every question multiple times to compensate for uncertainty. That is the strategy we use to obtain the upper bound. We can adapt Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy} (attaining Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-inaccurate} , see Appendix) or Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS}; it doesn't matter, since the new total amount of queries only depends, besides on $p$ and $\delta$, on the original amount of queries, which would be $nm$ for both algorithms. This also means we immediately have an upper bound for the problem over a matroid constraint.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:inaccurate_upper_bound}
In the query model with inaccurate query responses and parameter $p\in(0,\frac{1}{2})$, for any $\delta>0$, $n,m\in\mathbb{N}$, we attain a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution to Max Cover with probability at least $1-\delta$ using $nm\cdot U$ queries, where $U=\lceil -2\frac{\log((nm)/\delta)}{\log(4p(1-p))}\rceil$. This gives a query complexity in $\mathcal{O}(nm\log(nm/\delta))$.
\end{theorem}
We prove the lower bound using an information theoretic lower bound from multi-armed bandit theory. The proof disregards the possibility of matroid constraints, but since the original problem is a special case of the problem over a matroid constraint, the lower bound holds for both settings.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:inaccurate_lower_bound}
In the query model with inaccurate query responses, for any $\delta\in(0,1),\,n,m\in\mathbb{N}$, any algorithm that outputs an ($1-1/\e$)-approximate solution to Max Cover with probability at least $1-\delta$, makes $\Omega(nm\log(1/\delta))$ queries in expectation.
\end{theorem}

\section{Experiments}
Our experimental section is motivated by three questions. First, although our theoretical analysis (Theorems~\ref{thm:greedy_bound} and~\ref{thm:ls_bound}) provides good asymptotic bounds on the query complexity of both \textsc{Greedy} and \textsc{Local Search}-$\beta$ (Algorithms~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries} and~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}), large constant factors are a big influence for relatively small instances. For both theorems, the number of voters $\ell$ that needs to be sampled for each query set in order to arrive at the stated approximation ratio with high probability, is very large. This is not only impractical (each voter would need to reply more than $n$ times) but also makes the computations extremely slow. However, as the theoretical bounds are worst-case, we might be able to attain good results even on smaller instances. Second, Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_upper_bound} provides a good asymptotic bound on the number of queries required to compensate inaccurate voting behavior, requiring a number $U\in \mathcal{O}(nm/\delta)$ of repetitions for each query. We are interested in the experimental performance of our algorithms when we ignore this advice (that is, take $U=1$). Third and finally, we are interested in the performance of our algorithms when we combine incomplete \emph{and} inaccurate information. We did not study theoretically in this paper, but a naive approach for an upper bound would be to combine both separate bounds (by multiplication). What can we say about this experimentally?

We investigate these questions by running both our querying algorithms (Algorithms~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries} and~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}) and their exact versions (Algorithms~\ref{alg:greedy} and~\ref{alg:LS}) on 18 open-use datasets provided by Polis \footnote{https://github.com/compdemocracy/openData}, using $p=0$ and $p=\frac{1}{10}$. We used a committee size $k=8$ in all experiments (changing $k$ resulted in very similar results).

