We start with the full information case. In Figure~\ref{fig:exact_cc_scores} we show the Max Cover score, obtained by \textsc{Greedy} (Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy}) and \textsc{Local Search}-$\beta$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS}), per iteration. We took $\beta$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:ls_bound} (and will write \textsc{Local Search} going forward). We also show the Max Cover score that Approval Voting (selecting the $k$ most popular statements) would reach. Note that \textsc{Greedy} and Approval Voting each have a committee of size $i$ in iteration $i$, whereas \textsc{Local Search} starts with a random committee of size $k$, swapping one committee member each iteration. This explains the differences in scores for low iteration numbers. After $k=8$ iterations, we see that the score is confidently above $1-1/\e\approx 0.63$, indicating that the approximation ratio is achieved, even when the optimal score is 1. We also see that \textsc{Greedy} and \textsc{Local Search} perform better than Approval Voting, as expected.

Taking the scores of the \textsc{Greedy} and \textsc{Local Search} from Figure~\ref{fig:exact_cc_scores} as our baseline, we want to understand how close we can get to these scores when using only few queries, and/or when information is inaccurate. Recall that $t$ denotes the number of statements contained in a single query set. Taking $t=20$, we write $M$ for the expected number of query sets of size $t$ that each voter is given. We then configure \textsc{Local Search-queries} (Algorithm~\ref{alg:LS-incomplete-queries}), and \textsc{Greedy-queries} (Algorithm~\ref{alg:greedy-incomplete-queries}) so that $M=1,2,3,4,5$. This is far lower than what would be theoretically required. We do this for both $p=0$ and $p=\frac{1}{10}$. Figure~\ref{fig:query_scores} shows the performance of both algorithms relative to their exact versions. 

We see that both algorithms with accurate responses ($p=0$) obtain a score very close ($0.9-0.95$) to the exact versions of the algorithms, even when $M=1$. For the same number of queries, the local search algorithm seems to perform slightly closer to its exact version than the greedy algorithm does. Note that the exact versions of both algorithms obtained an average Max Cover score of around $0.73$. Multiplying by $0.9$ still yields a score above the worst-case approximation ratio of $1-1/\e\approx 0.63$, even when the optimal solution would have a Max Cover score of 1. Example~\ref{ex:experiments1} highlights the exceptional performance witnessed in the experiments for $p=0$.
\begin{example}\label{ex:experiments1}
The dataset \texttt{vtaiwan.uberx} has $m=197$ and $n=1921$. In Figure~\ref{fig:query_scores}, we see that, for $M=5$, the Max Cover score attained by \textsc{Local Search-queries}, on average across 18 data sets (and 20 runs per set) is approximately 0.95 of the score attained by \textsc{Local Search}. So, for our example, let's take $\gamma=0.95$. Then, for $k=8$ and $t=20$, taking $\delta=0.05$, we would need $M=7.109\cdot 10^8$.
\end{example}

Taking $p=\frac{1}{10}$, but with complete information, we make a similar observation. For both algorithms, adding an inaccuracy factor $p=\frac{1}{10}$ decreases the performance with a factor $0.95-0.97$ on average. Taking into consideration the performance of the exact versions of the algorithms, we see, like before, a score confidently above the worst-case approximation ratio of $1-1/\e$, whereas our theoretical results required taking multiple samples for each query $(i,j)$ to compensate for the inaccuracy. Example~\ref{ex:experiments2} shows a computation of the theoretically required values.
\begin{example}\label{ex:experiments2}
Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_upper_bound} provided us with a constant factor to multiply the number queries with in order to guarantee a $(1-1/\e)$-approximate solution with probability $\delta>0$. We compute $U=\lceil -2\frac{\log((nm)/\delta)}{\log(4p(1-p))}\rceil$, and taking again as example \texttt{vtaiwan.uberx}, with $m=197$ and $n=1921$. Then, for $\delta=0.05$, $U=32$. For our experiments, we used $U=1$.
\end{example}

Taking both $p=\frac{1}{10}$ \emph{and} incomplete information, not meeting the theoretically required number repetitions for \emph{both} scenarios, the performance takes a noticeable hit. These query algorithms perform at around $0.75-0.85$ of the exact versions of the algorithm, with a much bigger standard deviation. Remember, however, that we only queried a handful of statements per voter, and voters were possibly inaccurate, whereas the exact versions of the algorithms have complete access to all accurate information.

\section{Conclusion}
We studied approval-based committee elections with the goal of maximizing the Chamberlin-Courant score under incomplete or inaccurate information. The study of this type of multi-winner elections, and the focus on diversity, are motivated by applications in digital democracy for the support of online large-scale deliberation. 

Analytically, the paper determines bounds on the query complexity for the above settings, in particular: for the incomplete information setting, we establish 
upper bounds for adaptations to our setting of known algorithms for the Max Cover problem (Theorems Theorem~\ref{thm:ls_bound} and \ref{thm:greedy_bound}) 
and a lower bound (Theorem \ref{thm:incomplete_lower_bound}) for any non-adaptive algorithm. For the inaccurate information setting, we establish a lower bound for optimal approximations with high probability (Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_lower_bound}), as well as an upper bound using a greedy algorithm (Theorem~\ref{thm:inaccurate_upper_bound}). Empirically, we complement the worst-case results by testing the behavior of the algorithms on augmented real-world data from Polis. Even when voters respond, possibly inaccurately, to just a handful of statements, our algorithms perform well compared to the algorithms with complete and accurate access to each voters' ballots.

A first direction for future work is to query voters adaptively, to reduce the amount of queries required. Second, we hope to combine both incomplete and inaccurate information theoretically. Third, we wish to develop a more comprehensive theory of diversity, beyond the reliance on the Chamberlin-Courant score. Fourth, we aim to strengthen our experimental section by including synthetic data. Fifth, we intend to explore the significance of the above results also to applications in active learning \cite{settles2009active}, with the aim of optimizing the querying of a group annotators in distributed data labeling tasks (see, e.g., \cite{liscio2024value}). 

