Suppose that $\alg$ picked at least one of the candidates $c_{i,j}$ for $j\in\{1,\ldots,\ell-1\}$ for all $i$, then, like before, we need to pick at least $q$ designated candidates in order to attain the desired ratio. $\alg$ can do this by covering no $S_i$ and picking all $q$ designated candidates by chance, which happens, like proven above, with probability at most $p/l$. $\alg$ can also cover one of the sets $S_i$ and pick $q-1$ designated candidates by chance, or any other combination is possible up to covering all sets $S_i$. Then, for $t>1$, 
