where the inequality is due to the fact that covering a set $S_i$ is necessary to distinguish its designated candidate, but might not be sufficient. For $h=0$, we have bounded the probability above as $p/\ell$. Each of the other at most $(p-q)p$ probabilities is bounded from above by the probability of covering one set $S_i$, which is at most $\ell!2^{\ell+t} U p^{2}$. If we fix $U=\frac{\delta}{\ell!2^{t+\ell}p^3(p-q)}$ this becomes at most $\delta$. The total probability is then at most $\frac{p}{\ell} + \delta$. Recall that we want this probability to be at most $\epsilon$. We can achieve this, for instance, by fixing both terms to be $\epsilon/2$. Note that we choose $\delta$ (as it determines the query complexity), so it suffices to show that $\frac p\ell \leq \frac\epsilon2$. Recall that $p=2k-1$, so that this condition in met when $l\geq \frac{k-1}{\epsilon}$.

