\subsection{Overall Performance}
\noindent\textbf{Latency.}
As depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:overall-performance}(a), we evaluate the I/O latency per token and the corresponding speedup of \Neuralink{} on OnePlus 12. The results indicate that \Neuralink{} effectively mitigates the I/O bottleneck during LLM inference on smartphones, yielding speedups of up to $5.93\times$ over Llama.cpp and $3.23\times$ over LLMFlash. For OPT models, which exhibit high sparsity, \Neuralink{} achieves an average speedup of $2.23\times$ over LLMFlash across all model sizes and datasets. For denser models like Llama2-7B and Mistral-7B, optimizing I/O operations becomes much more challenging. However, with IOPS-oriented techniques, \Neuralink{} still achieves speedups of $13.8\%$ and 10.2\% over LLMFlash.

\noindent\textbf{Effective Bandwidth.}
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:overall-performance}(b). Consistent with the observed latency results, \Neuralink{} demonstrates a marked enhancement in bandwidth, achieving improvements of up to $4.32\times$ and $2.13\times$ over both two baselines, respectively. These gains primarily come from a substantial reduction in I/O operations due to more continuous access. By alleviating the device from IOPS constraints, the overall bandwidth utilization is boosted.

\subsection{Performance Breakdown}
Figure~\ref{fig:performance-breakdown} presents a detailed performance analysis of \Neuralink{}. The evaluation is conducted on two LLM families, OPT and Llama2, with OPT model sizes ranging from 350M to 6.7B. Using the strongest baseline, LLMFlash, as the start point, the results demonstrate that the offline and online stages of \Neuralink{} yield average performance improvements of $1.30\times$ and $1.26\times$, respectively, underscoring the effectiveness of both stages. By closely integrating both stages, \Neuralink{} achieves cumulative speedups of $1.68\times$ on average across all five models.

