\begin{abstract}
Federated Learning (FL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for leveraging diverse datasets from multiple sources while preserving data privacy by avoiding centralized storage. However, many existing approaches fail to account for the intricate gradient correlations between remote clients, a limitation that becomes especially problematic in data heterogeneity scenarios. In this work, we propose a novel FL framework utilizing Power-Norm Cosine Similarity (PNCS) to improve client selection for model aggregation. By capturing higher-order gradient moments, PNCS addresses non-IID data challenges, enhancing convergence speed and accuracy. Additionally, we introduce a simple algorithm ensuring diverse client selection through a selection history queue. Experiments with a VGG16 model across varied data partitions demonstrate consistent improvements over state-of-the-art methods.

% Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training across distributed clients while preserving data privacy. However, challenges such as data heterogeneity, communication costs, and privacy sensitivity hinder its effectiveness, especially in scenarios involving large foundation models like large language models. In this work, we propose a novel user selection strategy that addresses these challenges by leveraging Power-Norm Cosine Similarity as a robust metric for client selection. In addition, we introduce a simple Queue to encourage the model select diverse users to avoid model collapse. our model Power-Norm Cosine Similarity with Queue (PowCosQ), effectively captures gradient alignment under diverse training conditions, making it well-suited for both IID and non-IID data distributions.
% The heavy-tailed behavior of gradients, particularly in heterogeneous data settings, motivates the incorporation of higher-order moment statistics to enhance the informativeness of client selection. However, related works have shown that gradient similarity can lead to model collapse, as the model tends to repeatedly select the same clients. To address this issue, we propose a lightweight user index queue mechanism that promotes gradient diversity while maintaining communication efficiency. Furthermore, by leveraging the heavy-tailed nature of gradients, our method enables client selection using subsampled low-dimensional gradient representations, significantly reducing communication overhead.

% To prevent model collapse caused by repeatedly selecting similar clients, we introduce a simple yet effective user index queue that encourages gradient diversity without increasing communication costs. Additionally, we hypothesize that gradient norm distributions during FL training are heavy-tailed, motivating the need to capture higher-order moment statistics for more informative client selection. Through extensive numerical experiments, we demonstrate that our approach significantly improves convergence speed and final model accuracy compared to existing strategies. Our method is lightweight, scalable, and seamlessly integrates into existing FL protocols, making it a practical solution for real-world deployments.

\end{abstract}

