\begin{abstract}
Offshore wind farms (OWFs) are set to significantly contribute to global decarbonization efforts. Developers often use a sequential approach to optimize design variables and market participation for grid-integrated offshore wind farms. However, this method can lead to sub-optimal system performance, and uncertainties associated with renewable resources are often overlooked in decision-making. This paper proposes a control co-design approach, optimizing design and control decisions for integrating OWFs into the power grid while considering energy market and primary frequency market participation. Additionally, we introduce optimal sizing solutions for energy storage systems deployed onshore to enhance revenue for OWF developers over time. This framework addresses uncertainties related to wind resources and energy prices. We analyze five U.S. west-coast offshore wind farm locations and potential interconnection points, as identified by the Bureau of Ocean Energy Management (BOEM). Results show that optimized control co-design solutions can increase market revenue by 3.2\% and provide flexibility in managing wind resource uncertainties.
% Offshore wind farms (OWFs) are poised to play a significant role in global decarbonization efforts. To understand the efficient operation and design changes for power grid integrated offshore wind farm, developers considers a sequential approach. In this the developer first optimize for the relevant design variables and as second step optimizes for the market participation. The sequential approach can often result in the sub-optimal performance of the systems. In addition, the impact of uncertainty associated renewable resources are often unaccounted in the decision making. In this paper we propose the control co-design approach where we consider the offshore wind farm developer for optimizing the design, control decision on integrating the offshore wind farm to power grid, while considering the energy market participation. In addition to conventional energy market participation, we also consider the primary frequency market participation for the wind farm and optimize the associate control parameter. In this work we additionally introduce energy storage optimal sizing solutions for the offshore wind farm that can be deployed on onshore. The proposed framework consider the optimal operation of the energy storage that improvise the revenue for offshore wind farm developer in long run. We considered the uncertainties associated with the wind resource and energy prices. For the framework we consider the five U.S. west-coast offshore wind farm location analyzed and plausible point of interconnections by the Bureau of Ocean Energy Management (BOEM) study. The results shows that considering the baseline design of OWF the optimized control co-design solutions can provide a increase in the market revenue by 3.2\%. The onshore energy storage optimally tuned participation with OWF provide the required flexibility while considering wind resource uncertainties. 
\end{abstract}

